Right. I did not think of that.

However, I must say that I like the lawful quest best of the three. The
neutral one is too easy once you know how to deal with gas spores. The
chaotic one is outright difficult and can easily kill you. Of course,
you can simplify things a lot by genociding L... But if you do so before
seeing the quest the first time, you will still find a lot of nasties.
Of course, the chaotic quest contains a lot of useful loot, so it is
worth the effort.

The lawful quest is the only one that is structured. You can proceed in
several steps and you can interrupt at appropriate points. The altar
there is useful for sacrificing or praying. And that quest contains
parts that you can simply skip. If you have not genocided arch-liches,
you will of course never go close to the trap statue of an arch-lich. On
the other hand, in the flame-mage game in which I ascended recently (see
my post "YAAP - Flame mage [slashem]"), I got my magic protection from
that place, naturally only after having genocided L; but then I had the
appropriate scroll in my inventory from the beginning of the game. There
is a guaranteed set of gray dragon scales on the level, -9 and cursed,
of course, but this can be repaired. In fact, a single blessed scroll of
enchant armor is sufficient to get a piece of armor that has only the AC
of a cloak but still protects you against magic missiles and death rays.
(So at that stage it is comparable to a +0 cloak of magic resistance,
but with the potential to become much better.)

> I've never gotten even to mid-game with a Flame/Ice Mage. Congrats!

Thanks. They can become quite powerful, just as wizards, but they have
to survive the beginning of the game. A flame mage is a bit easier than
an ice mage, as he has a wand of fire in his initial inventory, which is
more useful than the wand of cold of the ice mage. Also, a hell-hound
pup as initial pet is something not to sniff at. Quite powerful, once it
has grown up.



